{"tool_name":"wise","contents":["biocontainers","bioschemas","debian","biotools","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"wise","identifiers":["biotools:wise","doi:10.1101/gr.10.4.547","doi:10.1101/gr.1865504"],"license":"GRL","summary":"Comparison of biopolymers, like dna and protein sequences","home_url":"http://www.ebi.ac.uk/~birney/wise2/","keywords":["3' Flanking Region","5' Flanking Region","Computational Biology","DNA, Complementary","Models, Theoretical","Predictive Value of Tests","Research Design","HMMs","DNA"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"wise","home":"http://www.ebi.ac.uk/Tools/Wise2/","summary":"Compare a protein sequences or protein HMMs to a genomic DNA sequence, allowing for introns and frameshifting errors.","addition_date":"2017-12-18T00:14:19Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T14:14:30.719416Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"version":["2.4.1"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:wise","credit":[{"email":"birney@ebi.ac.uk","name":"Ewan Birney","orcidid":"http://orcid.org/0000-0001-8314-8497"},{"typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/support/index.php"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/~birney/wise2/"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["jdispatcher"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Protein sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2479"}]},{"operation":[{"term":"Sequence alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0292"}]}],"name":"Wise2.4","operating_system":["Linux"],"owner":"mbs_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1101/gr.10.4.547","metadata":{"abstract":"The GeneWise method for combining gene prediction and homology searches was applied to the 2.9-Mb region from Drosophila melanogaster. The results from the Genome Annotation Assessment Project (GASP) showed that GeneWise provided reasonably accurate gene predictions. Further investigation indicates that many of the incorrect gene predictions from GeneWise were due to transposons with valid protein-coding genes and the remaining cases are pseudogenes or possible annotation oversights.","authors":[{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Durbin R."}],"citationCount":291,"date":"2000-04-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Genome Research","title":"Using GeneWise in the Drosophila annotation experiment"},"pmcid":"PMC310858","pmid":"10779496","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1101/gr.1865504","metadata":{"abstract":"We present two algorithms in this paper: GeneWise, which predicts gene structure using similar protein sequences, and Genomewise, which provides a gene structure final parse across cDNA- and EST-defined spliced structure. Both algorithms are heavily used by the Ensembl annotation system. The GeneWise algorithm was developed from a principled combination of hidden Markov models (HMMs). Both algorithms are highly accurate and can provide both accurate and complete gene structures when used with the correct evidence. \u00a92004 by Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory Press.","authors":[{"name":"Birney E."},{"name":"Clamp M."},{"name":"Durbin R."}],"citationCount":1927,"date":"2004-05-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Genome Research","title":"GeneWise and Genomewise"},"pmcid":"PMC479130","pmid":"15123596","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"DNA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0654"},{"term":"HMMs","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0160"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"Wise2.4","home":"https://bio.tools/wise","version":"2.4.1","summary":"Compare a protein sequences or protein HMMs to a genomic DNA sequence, allowing for introns and frameshifting errors.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:wise:2.4.1/cmd/www.ebi.ac.uk","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"packages":true,"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"g